:"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","producer_repository":"https://github.com/openai/math","sha256":"sha256:2c90a33675d6e8a37b8ab7c2e2107f358dd73fbc4c74dc1e183c17efa0e16267","source":"data/families.json"},"state":"producer-declared","value":{"rows":[{"label":"Nonadditive normalized $2$-quasitraces","statements":["KaplanskyQuasitrace"]},{"label":"Tensor-product failure of stable finiteness and quasitrace consequences","statements":["KaplanskyStableFiniteness"]}],"scope":"Kaplansky's quasitrace conjecture predicts that every $2$-quasitrace on a $C^*$-algebra is a trace. The formalization constructs a separable $C^*$-algebra with normalized $2$-quasitraces and fixed positive contractions $a,b$ for which every such quasitrace satisfies $\\mathrm{Re}(\\tau(a+b)-\\tau(a)-\\tau(b))\\ge1/144$. Thus none is additive on this pair.\n\nThe formalization also gives simple stably finite $C^*$-algebras whose spatial tensor product is properly infinite, a separable stably finite algebra with no tracial state, and a tensor-product example in which normalized $2$-quasitraces are lost. These are the stable-finiteness consequences named in the paper's title.","title":"Kaplansky's quasitrace conjecture and failure of tensor-product stable finiteness","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/294.md"}},"provenance":{"kind":"computed-navigation","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","repository":"https://github.com/openai/math","rule":"Projection of the pinned ledger; no new mathematical assessment."},"reader_comparison":{"headline_coverage":"agree","method":"Compare reported scope categories; notes remain attributed and are not adjudicated.","points":[{"definitions_to_check":null,"gap":null,"note":null,"reading":"reading:8ce43010410bb1d81168dabc","source_term":"MATCHES","state":"all"},{"definitions_to_check":"Hand-written quasitraces: `OneQuasitrace` (positivity, τ(x*x) = τ(xx*), real/imaginary additivity on self-adjoints, linearity on closed abelian subalgebras) and `IsTwo τ := ∃ σ : OneQuasitrace (M₂(A)), ∀ a, σ (upperLeft a) = τ a` (a 2-quasitrace as one that extends to M₂(A), rather than requiring τ ⊗ Tr₂ to be a quasitrace); `C*_r(F₂)` is built as `closure (adjoin (left translations))` on ℓ²(F₂), `ProperlyInfinite A := ∃ s t, s*s = 1 ∧ t*t = 1 ∧ s*t = 0`.","gap":null,"note":"`kaplansky_quasitrace_counterexample : MainTarget := ∃ separable C*-algebra A, (∃ τ : OneQuasitrace A, τ.Normalized ∧ τ.IsTwo) ∧ ∃ positive contractions a b, ∀ normalized 2-quasitraces τ, 1/144 ≤ Re(τ(a+b) - τ a - τ b)` (all normalized 2-quasitraces are non-additive), and `stable_finiteness_bundle := SimpleStableTensorCounterexample ∧ ReducedQuasitraceLoss ∧ TraceFreeStablyFinite` (C*_r(F₂) and a simple stably finite M whose spatial tensor product `tensorAlgebra ρ` is `ProperlyInfinite`, plus the quasitrace-loss and trace-free examples). Both parts of the summary are stated.","reading":"reading:2ddbd927aac9e55b946dad41","source_term":"full","state":"all"}],"readings":["reading:8ce43010410bb1d81168dabc","reading:2ddbd927aac9e55b946dad41"],"state":"agree"},"readings":[{"availability":"recorded","credit":{"atlas_pin":"755c4b729512c7e2c7d1d3303eb9706f5adf1681","authors":[{"handle":"carlostoledo1891","name":"Carlos Toledo"}],"kind":"third-party-reported","license":"MIT","method":"Every Comparator challenge re-run on Linux with nanoda switched on, decided by a preregistered rule; every family's challenge statements read against OpenAI's headline, clause by clause. Decider: Comparator (leanprover/comparator d03acab1 = tag v4.34.0, built with the release's toolchain v4.34.1), lean4export 076e8e57, landrun 811cfff5, nanoda_lib 3a240721 — on GitHub's Linux runners (.github/workflows/openai-math-kernel.yml), one j","name":"Carlos Toledo, cert-machine","pin":"9814e4c06281e34a113ce853d7d99ccce160b750","repository":"https://github.com/carlostoledo1891/cert-machine","source":"cert-machine","url":"https://carlostoledo.